Evaluating lox expressions we execute the syntax. Different interpreters do this step differently eg: convert to machine code, bytecode etc. What we will be doing is execute the python code in which our interpreter is written in to produce values. Python provides garbabe collection and is dynamically typed similar to how Lox is designed. Python also provides isinstance builtin function we can use to selectively execute the operations based on the operands, eg: concatanating two strings when + operator is used between two operands.
We will use the following mapping between python and Lox.
Lox                         Python
Any Lox value               object
nil                         None
Boolean                     bool
number                      float
string                      str
Python is not a statically typed language. As such the mapping will not be enforced by the language but we will use the typing functionality to encode our mapping.
In the book a design pattern - Visitor - is used to implement the logic of each types executing the operator. In Python we have match statement which allows us to implement the functionality of selectively executing block of code based on the type in a direct manner. We will be using that to implement the operations. This becomes a pretty significant evaluate method but is fairly readable and all the code lives in one function.
Evaluating literals is pretty straight forward. We simply pull out the value since it was already eagerly created in the parser when creating the nodes of the syntax tree.
Evaluating grouping requires evaluating inside the parenthesis. For this we return a recursive call to the sub-expression inside the parenthesis.
case Grouping(expr):
        return self.evaluate(expr)

Evaluating unary operator we evaluate the expression first (similar to grouping) and then apply the unary operator. For minus we make sure that the expression evaluates the number type and for ! we ensure the expression evaluates to a boolean type. In discussing boolean the concept of what evaluate to true and false needs to be clarified. Different languages have different ideas on what evaluates to true and false. In Lox we will only consider false and nil as fasly - meaning evaluates to false - and everything else will evaluate to true.
Evaluating binary operators multiply, divide and minus operators are straight forward. We evaluate the left and right operands; make sure they are of type number and return the value of resulting expression. For plus operator we will be supporting concatenation of string as well. This requires selectively checking both operands and then applying the appropriate operation. Since Python behaves the same way it is straight forward to implement it.
case TokenType.PLUS:
        if isinstance(left_value, str) and isinstance(right_value, str):
                return left_value + right_value
        if isinstance(left_value, float) and isinstance(
                right_value, float
        ):
                return left_value + right_value
Evaluating comparison operators &lt;, &lt;=, &gt; and &gt;= we check that the operands are of type number and evaluate the expression. For = and != we do not support any implicit conversions. Consequently 3 and "3" do not evaluate as equal. We implement this by ensuring that both operands are of the same types before checking the equality. If the type check fails the equality check immediately fails.
Runtime Error: Errors in parsing and scanning are caught by the Scanner and Parser class. There is another class of errors we need to consider which are runtime errors. There are errors that are not caught by the scanner and parser. For example it does not make sense to divide one string with another "undefined" / 3;. If we did not provide Lox specific error handling for operations like this the user would see error trace from Python which is not user friendly. We would like to provide Lox specific error handling. When the user is executing expressions using REPL we also would like to allow the user to continue in the REPL without exiting if one of the executions turns out to contain error.
We do this by creating Lox specific error handling class LoxRuntimeError which inherits from python builtin RuntimeError exception.
@staticmethod
def _require_number(operator: Token, value: object) -&gt; float:
        """Confirm that value is a float and return it."""
        if type(value) is float:
                return value
        raise LoxRuntimeError(operator, "Operand must be a number.")
Should a runtime error be detected we use the try/except Python functionality to gracefully inform the user that a runtime error has occurred.
def interpret(self, expression: Expr) -&gt; None:
        """Evaluate the expression and print the value."""
        try:
                value: object = self.evaluate(expression)
                print(self._stringify(value))
        except LoxRuntimeError as error:
                ErrorReporter.runtime_error(error)
The runtime_error function consumes the exception and prints a user friendly message specific to the error token.
The interpret method evaluates the expression and prints the result to standard output. The _stringify method contains string formatting logic specific Lox that differes from Python.
def _stringify(self, obj: object) -&gt; str:
        """Convert a Lox value to its display representation."""
        if obj is None:
                return "nil"
        if isinstance(obj, float):
                text = str(obj)
                if text.endswith(".0"):
                        return text[:-2]
        if isinstance(obj, bool):
                # Lox booleans are lowercase.
                text = str(obj)
                return text.lower()
        return str(obj)
That is all there is to it.

Upto this point we have implemented quite a bit of building blocks. In implementing the ability to make function calls we bring lot of those blocks together. This was a long chapter and has a lot of content. To do justice to the topic I highly recommend reading the book. In this write up I am going to focus not so much on the implementation but on the salient ideas that stand out to me during the implementation.
Syntax for a function is add(2, 3). The starting keyword - the callee / the function being called - in our Lox programming language add is an expression that gets evaluated. As such it could be an expression that evaluates to a function and is not part of the function call syntax. The elements within the parenthesis are arguments each of which are also expressions.
Function Currying refers to returning returning partial function which itself take additional parameters. It is easier to illustrate with an example. fn(1)(2)(3); here fn is a function that takes one argument and returns another function that takes one argument which also returns a function that takes one argument which finally completes the chain of calls and execution ends. This style of chaining series of function calls is Currying. Programming langauge Haskell has this concept baked into the language. We have that support in Lox.
def _call(self) -&gt; Expr:
        """Parse a function call."""
        expr: Expr = self._primary()

        while True:
                if self._match(TokenType.LEFT_PAREN):
                        expr = self._finish_call(expr)
                else:
                        break
        return expr

def _finish_call(self, callee: Expr) -&gt; Expr:
        arguments: List[Expr] = []
        if not self._check(TokenType.RIGHT_PAREN):
                arguments.append(self._expression())
                while self._match(TokenType.COMMA):
                        if len(arguments) &gt;= MAX_ARGUMENTS:
                                ErrorReporter.error_at_token(
                                        self._peek(),
                                        f"Can't have more than {MAX_ARGUMENTS} arguments.",
                                )
                        arguments.append(self._expression())
        paren: Token = self._consume(
                TokenType.RIGHT_PAREN, "Expect ')' after arguments."
        )
        return Call(callee=callee, paren=paren, arguments=arguments)
In the preceeding lines of code we have the logic of how Currying is implemented. Call is the AST node for the function call.
Capping the maximum number of arguments to be allowed in a funciton call. I never thought about this when using a language. In the Lox language we are restricting it to 255 arguments. Above that and we report an error; we do not terminate the program however. So, nothing stops a user from passing more arguments but the warning will be displayed.
What to do when a function is called with too many arguments? Looks like differnt languages handle this differently. JavaScript allows it and discards the excess parameters and fills in undefined value for missing arguments. Since Lox is a dynamic typed language we don’t have static type checking so we follow python (dynamic language) and raise an Error when there is a mismatch in the number of parameters expected and received in a function call. Technical term for this is the “arity” of a function - expected number of arguments.
Native functions are functions that have operations implemented in the underlying language - in our case Python. These are part of the language and are made availabe to the user as convenient way to do useful things. For our case we implement a clock function which return number of seconds elapsed since a specific time. Native functions allow the user to do simple things without jumping through hoops for things like reading files etc.
Local function and Closure.
Lox supports local functions. Local functions are functions declared and used inside another function. Below is an illustration from the book:
fun makeCounter() {
  var i = 0;
  fun count() {
    i = i + 1;
    print i;
  }

  return count;
}

var counter = makeCounter();
counter(); // "1".
counter(); // "2".
Here makeCounter returns a reference to the count local function. In order for this above example to work correctly the state of the local variable i needs to be preserved and updated. This idea of a reference function that wraps local variable reference is called Closure as an function closes over the variables.
We accomplish this by creating an Environment - self.closure in below snippet - that captures the state of the function when it is declared. And when the function is called we refer to this environment instead of the global environment.
class LoxFunction(LoxCallable):
    def __init__(self, declaration: Function, closure: Environment) -&gt; None:
        self.declaration: Function = declaration
        self.closure: Environment = closure

    def call(self, interpreter: Interpreter, arguments: List[object]) -&gt; object:
        environment: Environment = Environment(enclosing=self.closure)
        for parameter, argument in zip(self.declaration.params, arguments):
            environment.define(parameter.lexeme, argument)

        try:
            interpreter.execute_block(self.declaration.body, environment)
        except Return as return_signal:
            return return_signal.value
        return None
That is all I have to say about that.

In this post I am going to do a shallow walkthrough of the Parser class which implements the parsing logic for the interpreter. Parser consumes the list of tokens created by the Scanner class and produces a syntax tree or Abstract Syntax Tree based on the grammar of the language.
In this section we are only going to talk about expressions (not statements). Simple way to think of expression is that it evaluates to a value. statements do things like print a string. The rules of the grammar dictate valid expressions. Our Parser generates error messages for illformed expressions.
Here is an example of a simple grammar that can be used to create expressions.
expression     → literal | unary | binary | grouping ;
literal        → NUMBER | STRING | "true" | "false" | "nil" ;
grouping       → "(" expression ")" ;
unary          → ( "-" | "!" ) expression ;
binary         → expression operator expression ;
operator       → "==" | "!=" | "&lt;" | "&lt;=" | "&gt;" | "&gt;=" | "+"  | "-"  | "*" | "/" ;
Though simple to create expressions the issue we run into is ambiguity. We can use the same grammar to produce both (6 / 3) - 1 or 6 / (3 - 1). This is not good for our parser because based on the rules it can’t identify which one is the correct one. We fix this by using operator precedence and associativity which makes the grammar complex but removes the ambiguity issue.
Here is the new grammar:
expression     → equality ;
equality       → comparison ( ( "!=" | "==" ) comparison )* ;
comparison     → term ( ( "&gt;" | "&gt;=" | "&lt;" | "&lt;=" ) term )* ;
term           → factor ( ( "-" | "+" ) factor )* ;
factor         → unary ( ( "/" | "*" ) unary )* ;
unary          → ( "!" | "-" ) unary
               | primary ;
primary        → NUMBER | STRING | "true" | "false" | "nil"
               | "(" expression ")" ;
The operator precedence increases as we go down the list. So unary has higher precedence than comparison. This also makes intuitive sense. In -1 == 2 we expect -1 to take higher precedence than ==. The grammar rules are implemented as functions where some are recursive in themselves (like unary) or through other functions (like expression which can fall through to primary). The grammar rules with * are the binary operators and encode associativity.
Here we take a look at one of the rules which is implemented as a function. Recursive Descent parsing is the technique used to chain the function calls and implement the parser.
def _comparison(self) -&gt; Expr:
        """Parse comparison operators (&lt;, &lt;=, &gt;, &gt;=) left-associatively."""
        expr: Expr = self._term()
        expr = self._binary_helper(
                expr,
                self._term,
                TokenType.LESS,
                TokenType.LESS_EQUAL,
                TokenType.GREATER,
                TokenType.GREATER_EQUAL,
        )
        return expr 

def _binary_helper(
        self, expr: Expr, parse_operand: Callable[[], Expr], *types: TokenType
) -&gt; Expr:
        """Parse a left-associative chain of binary operators using parse_operand for each operand."""
        while self._match(*types):
                operator: Token = self._previous()
                right: Expr = parse_operand()
                expr = Binary(expr, operator, right)
        return expr
As specified by the rule, first thing to do is evaluate the higher precedence token sequence (if any) which is done in self._term() function call. After that we make use of the _binary_helper method to create a binary expression accounting for associativity in the case of sequence of operators. We do this with the while loop.
The token streams are consumed by the functions and appropriate nodes in the syntax tree are created.
But ofcourse we have to talk about what happens when something goes wrong in the event that the token sequence are illformed. In that case we have error reporting functions that provide high level clue on where in the file the potential error exists and possibly clues on which token.
And pretty much covers at a high level the operations of the Parser class. Main objectives are to consume the token stream and create a Abstract Syntax Tree and provide decent error reporting to help the user correct the code.

Next chapter is about adding state and statements. The implementation so far has expressions which evaluate to a value. Statements allow us to do more like print outputs, update state etc. In order to take advantage of updating state via statements we also add the idea of a state. This allows the ability to create variables that refer to certain expressions which can later be recalled.
Example of state:
var foo = 23;
print foo; // this should print 23
To start we add two types of statements which are print statement - allows us the ability to output stuff in the terminal - and expression statement - allows us the ability to execute expressions that have side effect like function calls that have side effect. Implementing functions is done in future chapter.
In order to accomodate statements we update the grammar of Lox.
program        → statement* EOF ;

statement      → exprStmt
               | printStmt ;

exprStmt       → expression ";" ;
printStmt      → "print" expression ";" ;
Where previously we only had expression, the updated grammar has program at the highest level which consists of one or more statements. statement consist of the two types of statements we talked about earlier. exprStmt is any valid expression that ends with ;. 2 + 2; is a valid statement though not very useful as we are not doing anything with the computation. printStmt allows us to output values in the standard output. Based on the grammar printStmt takes in any expression evaluates it and prints the value.
parser contains implementation to process the statements. parser now returns a list of statements in line with the updated grammar. By default everything is an expression statement. If a statement starts with print key word then the expression after the print statement is evaluated.
With this we have the ability to fire the REPL and start doing print 2; and expect it to work.
Next is declaring and accessing variables. The syntax used to declare variable is var foo = 23; - variable declaration statement. Since we don’t want statements like if (some_boolean_logic) var foo = 23; which adds unnecessary complexity we separate variable declaration statement from rest of the statements. Since any statement can be placed where variable declaration statements (not vice versa) we make the following udpate to the grammar. If a statement is not a variable declaration then it falls through to rest of the statements.
program        → declaration* EOF ;

declaration    → varDecl
               | statement ;

statement      → exprStmt
               | printStmt ;
When parser sees the var key word then it creates a Var node with the associated name and initializer value. If the initializer value is not passed the initializer value defaults to python None. In order to identify a previously declared variable we add some logic to our parser class.
// If an identifier is found a Variable node is created with its associated field
if self._match(TokenType.IDENTIFIER):
        return Variable(self._previous())
With these updates we have added the ability to create and referrence variables.
Next stop is creating the Environment where the variable mappings are saved. I am not going to go into the details of the choices and would highly recommend reading the book. Two choices made: redefining variables are ok at the global level. Referring to variables that do not exist is not a compile time but a runtime error. The data structure used for the implementation is a python dictionary. We instantiate an Environment class for each session of the interpreter. The class contains the dictionary and methods to update and access the dictionary. Variable declaration syntax translates to adding a mapping and variable referrence translates to looking up the value of the variable.
Lox supports variable assignment. Variable assignment is an expression so evaluates to a value. To accomodate this we update the grammar especially the expression.
expression     → assignment ;
assignment     → IDENTIFIER "=" assignment
               | equality ;
Assignment operation itself is a bit more complex. This is because the not only must the right side be evaluated for the value the left must be as well. And the left side can also be a complex type instead of a variable (especially in the future when we support class). The solution in a nutshell is to evaluate the left side of the = and check to see if it of the right type (Variable) and if so move forward with the assignment operation. A new Assign node is created that contains the name of the variable and value. Key difference between assignment and variable decleration is that assignment doesn’t create a new variable if it doesn’t already exist and throws runtime error. With this have global variables.
Scoping is the idea that certain objects exist and are available in certain parts of the code and not others. Being a close cousion of C Lox supports block scope with curly braces {}. The way we implement the scoping idea is by creating a new Environment for the scope which will have its nested parents to refer to (should it have them). This way when a variable is referenced it is not only checked in the current environment (dictionary of mappings) but also the chain of parent environment. This way we can refer to a variable that existed in a broader scope but also have a ability to overwrite it within the “local” scope.
Lot of details have been brushed over as this is just a quick brain dump of the chapter. If you are really interested I would highly recommend reading the book.
